22:52:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: A jacket can be both waterproof and breathable thanks to specially engineered membranes or coatings that exploit the size difference between liquid water droplets and water vapor molecules. The core idea is a **microporous structure**. The material contains billions of tiny pores per square inch. These pores are: - **Smaller than a raindrop** (around 100–5000 microns), so liquid water cannot penetrate. - **Larger than a single water vapor molecule** (about 0.0004 microns), so sweat vapor from your body can escape. Common approaches include: - **PTFE membranes** (e.g., Gore-Tex): A thin film of expanded polytetrafluoroethylene with a node-and-fibril structure that creates the micropores. It’s often laminated to an outer fabric and backed by a protective liner. - **Polyurethane coatings**: A continuous, non-porous film that is hydrophilic (water-loving). Instead of pores, it works by molecular diffusion—moisture vapor is absorbed on the inside, transported through the polymer chains, and released on the outside. - **Nanoporous membranes**: Newer variants use electrospun nanofibers or other nanostructures to achieve the same size-selective effect with improved breathability. A **Durable Water Repellent (DWR) finish** is usually applied to the outer fabric. It makes water bead up and roll off, preventing the face fabric from “wetting out” (becoming saturated), which would block the pores and stop breathability. So the combination of a microporous or molecular-diffusion membrane and a DWR coating allows liquid water to stay out while water vapor escapes, keeping you dry from both rain and sweat.
